WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.438
Mock Interviews.

2
00:00:01.718 --> 00:00:05.601
Chapter 60, from the Interview part of the System Design Playbook.

3
00:00:07.351 --> 00:00:08.311
At a glance:

4
00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:09.764
Chapter at a glance.

5
00:00:10.044 --> 00:00:11.047
Reader bands:

6
00:00:11.327 --> 00:00:13.631
B1, then B4, Prerequisites:

7
00:00:13.911 --> 00:00:17.601
Ch 57, Ch 58, Ch 59.

8
00:00:17.881 --> 00:00:18.799
What you get:

9
00:00:19.079 --> 00:00:28.209
How to run mock interviews that actually change your performance: formats, the interviewer's script, pacing, debriefs, calibration and tracking progress.

10
00:00:28.489 --> 00:00:34.100
Four full worked mocks with interviewer cards, timelines, transcript excerpts and scorecards:

11
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.812
A coupon redemption (B1, B2).

12
00:00:37.092 --> 00:00:39.759
B order-status notifications (B2).

13
00:00:41.109 --> 00:00:44.607
C dark-store inventory for 10-minute delivery (B3).

14
00:00:44.887 --> 00:00:48.087
D taking the platform to a second country (B4).

15
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:49.626
What the labs measured:

16
00:00:49.906 --> 00:00:58.482
L1 (pacing): in six recorded practice mocks, the deep-dive and failure phases got 11.2 of a planned 16 minutes.

17
00:00:58.762 --> 00:01:04.821
A single checkpoint rule (drawing the HLD by minute 20) recovers them to 15.2.

18
00:01:05.101 --> 00:01:07.767
It does not fix an 11-minute clarify phase.

19
00:01:08.047 --> 00:01:10.223
L2 (calibration), simulated:

20
00:01:11.573 --> 00:01:20.704
One calibrated interviewer decides as accurately as four uncalibrated ones (86.4% versus 86.5%).

21
00:01:20.984 --> 00:01:26.381
Two uncalibrated interviewers reach the same verdict only 68% of the time.

22
00:01:26.661 --> 00:01:33.360
A candidate just above the bar gets a hire from one uncalibrated mock only 60% of the time.

23
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:51.773
L3 (check a claim before the mock), real PostgreSQL: a single-row conditional UPDATE gives 4,398 reservations per second with one thread, but only ~2,000 per second at 32 threads with a 77 ms p99.

24
00:01:52.053 --> 00:01:57.323
Sixteen bucket rows give 10,844 per second at 8 threads.

25
00:01:58.673 --> 00:02:09.297
L4 (progress), simulated: even when you really improve by 0.10 per mock, one in three candidates scores lower on mock 4 than on mock 1.

26
00:02:09.577 --> 00:02:12.307
A statistical trend needs about 12 mocks.

27
00:02:12.587 --> 00:02:13.569
Reading time:

28
00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:14.851
70 min.

29
00:02:16.201 --> 00:02:17.055
Part 1.

30
00:02:17.335 --> 00:02:19.255
Introduction and Why It Matters.

31
00:02:20.505 --> 00:02:22.083
A story: the fifth mock.

32
00:02:22.363 --> 00:02:24.390
Meera had done four mocks with friends.

33
00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.659
All four went fine, and she was nervous about each real loop anyway.

34
00:02:28.939 --> 00:02:34.379
On the fifth, her friend recorded the session and then sat down with her, the recording and a stopwatch.

35
00:02:34.659 --> 00:02:35.875
The recording showed:

36
00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:37.115
Requirements:

37
00:02:37.395 --> 00:02:39.742
11 minutes of clarifying questions.

38
00:02:40.022 --> 00:02:40.982
Deep dive:

39
00:02:41.262 --> 00:02:43.203
3 minutes, because time ran out.

40
00:02:43.483 --> 00:02:45.659
Failure handling: never discussed.

41
00:02:45.939 --> 00:02:52.169
Two claims she couldn't back up: a Redis counter handles any load and Kafka guarantees exactly-once.

42
00:02:53.519 --> 00:03:01.028
None of her four earlier mocks had surfaced this, because the friend had been friendly: no clock, no follow-ups, no scorecard.

43
00:03:01.308 --> 00:03:05.596
A mock is only useful if it is harder and more honest than the real thing.

44
00:03:05.876 --> 00:03:17.140
That means a real clock, an interviewer with a script and hidden requirements, follow-up chains, a scorecard against a level, a recording, and a debrief that ends with two things to change.

45
00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.047
This chapter is how to run those, on both sides of the table.

46
00:03:22.397 --> 00:03:23.079
Quote:

47
00:03:23.359 --> 00:03:25.023
The thesis of this chapter.

48
00:03:25.303 --> 00:03:36.695
Run mocks as experiments: a timed prompt with an interviewer card;. follow-up chains to the edge of what you know;. a level-anchored scorecard;. a recorded debrief with two changes.

49
00:03:36.975 --> 00:03:38.831
Pace with checkpoints (L1).

50
00:03:39.111 --> 00:03:42.482
Calibrate peer interviewers before trusting a verdict (L2).

51
00:03:42.762 --> 00:03:45.855
Check your standard claims against real numbers (L3).

52
00:03:46.135 --> 00:03:50.082
Judge progress on trends and behaviours, not one score (L4).

53
00:03:51.432 --> 00:03:52.755
Learning objectives.

54
00:03:53.035 --> 00:03:54.933
By the end you will be able to:

55
00:03:55.213 --> 00:04:00.120
Run a mock as candidate and as interviewer, with a card, a clock and a scorecard.

56
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.240
Pace 45 minutes with checkpoints, and recover when you're behind.

57
00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.891
Handle pushback, being stuck, and what if chains gracefully.

58
00:04:08.171 --> 00:04:10.837
Debrief into specific, behavioural changes.

59
00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:14.061
Calibrate a peer group and track progress honestly.

60
00:04:15.411 --> 00:04:16.286
Part 2.

61
00:04:16.566 --> 00:04:18.443
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

62
00:04:19.693 --> 00:04:21.656
Intuition: a dress rehearsal.

63
00:04:21.936 --> 00:04:26.267
Actors rehearse in costume, on stage, with lights, before opening night.

64
00:04:26.547 --> 00:04:32.328
Mistakes that would be fatal on the night (a missed cue, a prop in the wrong place) are cheap in rehearsal.

65
00:04:32.608 --> 00:04:34.336
A mock is your dress rehearsal.

66
00:04:34.616 --> 00:04:41.976
It is only useful if the conditions match: the time limit, a stranger's questions, a whiteboard, and nobody rescuing you.

67
00:04:43.326 --> 00:04:44.393
Mock formats.

68
00:04:44.673 --> 00:04:46.017
Here is a quick table:

69
00:04:46.297 --> 00:04:49.091
Format, and Good for, and Limits, and Frequency.

70
00:04:49.371 --> 00:04:50.630
Solo, recorded.

71
00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:51.742
Good for:

72
00:04:52.022 --> 00:04:54.049
Pacing, clarity, speaking aloud.

73
00:04:54.329 --> 00:04:55.161
Limits:

74
00:04:55.441 --> 00:04:57.169
No follow-ups, no pushback.

75
00:04:57.449 --> 00:04:58.387
Frequency:

76
00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:00.075
2 or week early on.

77
00:05:00.355 --> 00:05:01.635
Peer (swap roles).

78
00:05:01.915 --> 00:05:02.747
Good for:

79
00:05:03.027 --> 00:05:04.862
Follow-ups, both perspectives.

80
00:05:05.142 --> 00:05:05.974
Limits:

81
00:05:06.254 --> 00:05:08.174
Calibration varies (L2).

82
00:05:08.454 --> 00:05:09.393
Frequency:

83
00:05:09.673 --> 00:05:10.995
1 to 2 or week.

84
00:05:11.275 --> 00:05:12.918
Senior colleague or mentor.

85
00:05:13.198 --> 00:05:14.030
Good for:

86
00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:16.209
Level-accurate probing, stories.

87
00:05:16.489 --> 00:05:17.321
Limits:

88
00:05:17.601 --> 00:05:18.433
Their time.

89
00:05:18.713 --> 00:05:19.651
Frequency:

90
00:05:19.931 --> 00:05:21.147
2 to 4 total.

91
00:05:21.427 --> 00:05:22.750
Paid or professional.

92
00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:23.862
Good for:

93
00:05:24.142 --> 00:05:26.723
Calibrated scoring, company-specific style.

94
00:05:27.003 --> 00:05:27.835
Limits:

95
00:05:28.115 --> 00:05:28.841
Cost.

96
00:05:29.121 --> 00:05:30.059
Frequency:

97
00:05:30.339 --> 00:05:32.046
1 to 2 before key loops.

98
00:05:32.326 --> 00:05:33.755
Real low-stakes loops.

99
00:05:34.035 --> 00:05:34.867
Good for:

100
00:05:35.147 --> 00:05:36.321
Real conditions.

101
00:05:36.601 --> 00:05:37.433
Limits:

102
00:05:37.713 --> 00:05:38.779
Cost of a no.

103
00:05:39.059 --> 00:05:39.998
Frequency:

104
00:05:40.278 --> 00:05:41.131
Optional.

105
00:05:42.481 --> 00:05:50.119
Order: solo first to fix pacing, then peers for follow-ups, then calibrated interviewers to find out where you actually stand.

106
00:05:51.469 --> 00:05:52.706
The interviewer card.

107
00:05:52.986 --> 00:05:55.354
A mock interviewer needs more than a prompt.

108
00:05:55.634 --> 00:06:00.157
They need a card holding what a real interviewer knows and the candidate doesn't:

109
00:06:01.507 --> 00:06:02.851
Here is a quick table:

110
00:06:03.131 --> 00:06:04.880
Card section, and Contents.

111
00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:05.928
Prompt:

112
00:06:06.208 --> 00:06:08.213
One sentence, deliberately vague.

113
00:06:08.493 --> 00:06:09.688
Hidden requirements:

114
00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:14.939
Answers to reveal only if asked (scale, latency, regions, consistency).

115
00:06:15.219 --> 00:06:16.136
Must-cover:

116
00:06:16.416 --> 00:06:19.189
The 2 to 3 hard parts the design must address.

117
00:06:19.469 --> 00:06:20.664
Follow-up chains:

118
00:06:20.944 --> 00:06:26.640
3 to 4 chains, each 3 to 4 links deep (Ch 59 §2.4).

119
00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:27.880
Curveballs:

120
00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.848
One requirement change at minute ~30.

121
00:06:31.128 --> 00:06:32.237
Level anchors:

122
00:06:32.517 --> 00:06:37.744
What B2 or B3 or B4 answers sound like for this prompt (Ch 58).

123
00:06:38.024 --> 00:06:38.941
Scorecard:

124
00:06:39.221 --> 00:06:43.189
Dimensions and 1 to 4 anchors (Ch 57 L4).

125
00:06:44.539 --> 00:06:50.662
The four mocks in §2.6, §2.9 each come with a card you can reuse.

126
00:06:52.012 --> 00:06:53.377
The interviewer's script.

127
00:06:53.657 --> 00:06:54.937
0 to 1 min:

128
00:06:55.217 --> 00:06:56.391
Deliver the prompt.

129
00:06:56.671 --> 00:06:57.737
Say nothing else.

130
00:06:58.017 --> 00:06:59.105
1 to 10 min:

131
00:06:59.385 --> 00:07:01.412
Answer questions from the card only.

132
00:07:01.692 --> 00:07:03.783
Unasked hidden requirements stay hidden.

133
00:07:04.063 --> 00:07:04.895
Throughout:

134
00:07:05.175 --> 00:07:09.612
Take timestamped notes of phases, claims and numbers. ~20 min:

135
00:07:09.892 --> 00:07:12.964
If the candidate is still on requirements, don't rescue them.

136
00:07:13.244 --> 00:07:14.033
Note it.

137
00:07:14.313 --> 00:07:15.956
20 to 35 min:

138
00:07:16.236 --> 00:07:22.785
Pull one follow-up chain per hard part until the candidate reaches the edge of what they know. ~30 min:

139
00:07:23.065 --> 00:07:24.431
Deliver the curveball.

140
00:07:25.781 --> 00:07:27.402
40 to 45 min:

141
00:07:27.682 --> 00:07:30.647
What would you do next with more time? and questions to you.

142
00:07:30.927 --> 00:07:31.738
After:

143
00:07:32.018 --> 00:07:36.413
Fill the scorecard before discussing it, so the discussion can't soften the score.

144
00:07:36.693 --> 00:07:39.253
Then debrief (§2.10).

145
00:07:40.603 --> 00:07:42.075
Behaviours under pressure.

146
00:07:42.355 --> 00:07:43.699
Here is a quick table:

147
00:07:43.979 --> 00:07:47.072
Situation, and Weak response, and Strong response.

148
00:07:47.352 --> 00:07:48.099
Stuck.

149
00:07:48.379 --> 00:07:49.467
Weak response:

150
00:07:49.747 --> 00:07:51.688
Silence, or random components.

151
00:07:51.968 --> 00:07:53.141
Strong response:

152
00:07:53.421 --> 00:07:58.584
Let me reason from the requirement: we need X within Y, so the options are A or B...

153
00:07:58.864 --> 00:08:00.229
Pushback (why not X?).

154
00:08:00.509 --> 00:08:01.597
Weak response:

155
00:08:01.877 --> 00:08:04.224
Abandon the choice, or defend blindly.

156
00:08:04.504 --> 00:08:05.677
Strong response:

157
00:08:05.957 --> 00:08:10.331
Restate the requirement, compare X honestly, keep or switch with a reason.

158
00:08:10.611 --> 00:08:11.677
Unknown fact.

159
00:08:11.957 --> 00:08:13.045
Weak response:

160
00:08:13.325 --> 00:08:14.072
Bluff.

161
00:08:14.352 --> 00:08:15.525
Strong response:

162
00:08:15.805 --> 00:08:17.363
I don't know the exact number.

163
00:08:17.643 --> 00:08:18.624
I'd measure it.

164
00:08:18.904 --> 00:08:20.931
My estimate is ~N because...

165
00:08:21.211 --> 00:08:22.171
Wrong earlier.

166
00:08:22.451 --> 00:08:23.539
Weak response:

167
00:08:23.819 --> 00:08:24.587
Hide it.

168
00:08:24.867 --> 00:08:26.040
Strong response:

169
00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:27.664
I said Y earlier.

170
00:08:27.944 --> 00:08:29.245
That's wrong because Z.

171
00:08:29.525 --> 00:08:30.933
Let me fix the design.

172
00:08:31.213 --> 00:08:32.067
Curveball.

173
00:08:32.347 --> 00:08:33.435
Weak response:

174
00:08:33.715 --> 00:08:34.675
Start over.

175
00:08:34.955 --> 00:08:36.128
Strong response:

176
00:08:36.408 --> 00:08:40.781
Identify what changes, modify the minimum, re-check the invariants.

177
00:08:42.131 --> 00:08:43.241
Behind on time.

178
00:08:43.521 --> 00:08:44.609
Weak response:

179
00:08:44.889 --> 00:08:45.955
Rush everything.

180
00:08:46.235 --> 00:08:47.409
Strong response:

181
00:08:47.689 --> 00:08:52.297
Say so, compress the low-value phases aloud (L1), protect the deep dive.

182
00:08:52.577 --> 00:08:53.771
Interviewer silent.

183
00:08:54.051 --> 00:08:55.139
Weak response:

184
00:08:55.419 --> 00:08:56.763
Keep adding boxes.

185
00:08:57.043 --> 00:08:58.217
Strong response:

186
00:08:58.497 --> 00:08:59.307
Check in:

187
00:08:59.587 --> 00:09:02.425
Would you like me to go deeper on X or move to Y?

188
00:09:03.775 --> 00:09:05.887
Mock A, Coupon redemption service.

189
00:09:06.167 --> 00:09:07.041
Example:

190
00:09:07.321 --> 00:09:08.900
Interviewer card, Mock A.

191
00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:09.948
Prompt:

192
00:09:10.228 --> 00:09:14.303
Design a service that lets customers apply coupon codes at checkout.

193
00:09:14.583 --> 00:09:17.164
Hidden requirements (reveal only if asked):

194
00:09:17.444 --> 00:09:20.473
20M users and 50k active coupons.

195
00:09:20.753 --> 00:09:24.103
Peak 3,000 checkouts per second during sale events.

196
00:09:24.383 --> 00:09:31.380
Some coupons are single-use per user; others have a global cap, for example, the first 10,000 redemptions.

197
00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:36.353
Coupon validation must answer in p99 at most 50 ms.

198
00:09:37.703 --> 00:09:41.885
A redeemed coupon must never be redeemed twice, even with retries.

199
00:09:42.165 --> 00:09:43.082
Must-cover:

200
00:09:43.362 --> 00:09:45.901
A validation versus redemption split.

201
00:09:46.181 --> 00:09:49.445
Enforcing per-user and global caps under concurrency.

202
00:09:49.725 --> 00:09:52.093
Idempotent redemption tied to the order.

203
00:09:52.373 --> 00:09:53.205
Chains:

204
00:09:54.555 --> 00:10:02.085
The global cap at 3,000 per second, then a hot counter, then buckets or reservations, then over-issue tolerance.

205
00:10:02.365 --> 00:10:07.379
Payment fails after redemption, then release, then timeout sweeper, then idempotency.

206
00:10:07.659 --> 00:10:12.416
Stacking rules, then rule engine, then evaluation order (Ch 49).

207
00:10:12.696 --> 00:10:14.125
Curveball (min 30):

208
00:10:14.405 --> 00:10:18.587
Marketing wants a coupon valid only for the first order in each city.

209
00:10:18.867 --> 00:10:19.976
Level anchors:

210
00:10:20.256 --> 00:10:23.499
B1: a working CRUD design with a unique constraint.

211
00:10:24.849 --> 00:10:29.137
B2: caps under concurrency, idempotency and release on failure.

212
00:10:29.417 --> 00:10:33.641
B3: hot-cap handling with numbers, and an abuse or fraud angle.

213
00:10:34.991 --> 00:10:36.975
Timeline of a strong B2 answer:

214
00:10:38.325 --> 00:10:39.669
Here is a quick table:

215
00:10:39.949 --> 00:10:41.954
Min, and Phase, and What happened.

216
00:10:42.234 --> 00:10:43.343
0 to 5.

217
00:10:43.623 --> 00:10:44.477
Phase:

218
00:10:44.757 --> 00:10:45.695
Clarify.

219
00:10:45.975 --> 00:10:46.893
What happened:

220
00:10:47.173 --> 00:10:53.082
Asked scale, cap types, latency, stacking and what redeemed means (at payment, not at apply).

221
00:10:53.362 --> 00:10:54.450
5 to 8.

222
00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:55.583
Phase:

223
00:10:55.863 --> 00:10:56.717
Estimate.

224
00:10:56.997 --> 00:10:57.914
What happened:

225
00:10:58.194 --> 00:11:05.639
3,000 checkouts per second times ~30% with a coupon about 1,000 validations per second.

226
00:11:05.919 --> 00:11:08.138
One sale coupon can take most of them.

227
00:11:08.418 --> 00:11:09.549
8 to 11.

228
00:11:09.829 --> 00:11:10.682
Phase:

229
00:11:10.962 --> 00:11:11.922
API.

230
00:11:12.202 --> 00:11:13.119
What happened:

231
00:11:13.399 --> 00:11:19.778
POST or coupons or validate code, cartId returns the discount and a validation token.

232
00:11:20.058 --> 00:11:25.199
POST or coupons or redeem code, orderId is idempotent on the order id.

233
00:11:25.479 --> 00:11:26.759
11 to 15.

234
00:11:27.039 --> 00:11:27.893
Phase:

235
00:11:28.173 --> 00:11:28.983
Data.

236
00:11:29.263 --> 00:11:39.525
What happened: coupon (rules, caps), redemption (couponid, userid, orderid, status) with UNIQUE(couponid, userid) for single-use coupons and UNIQUE(orderid).

237
00:11:39.805 --> 00:11:41.341
15 to 24.

238
00:11:41.621 --> 00:11:42.474
Phase:

239
00:11:42.754 --> 00:11:43.714
HLD.

240
00:11:43.994 --> 00:11:44.911
What happened:

241
00:11:45.191 --> 00:11:52.658
Checkout calls validate (reads from a cached rules table), then reserves at order creation, then confirms on payment.

242
00:11:54.008 --> 00:11:55.544
24 to 36.

243
00:11:55.824 --> 00:11:56.677
Phase:

244
00:11:56.957 --> 00:11:57.917
Deep dive.

245
00:11:58.197 --> 00:11:59.115
What happened:

246
00:11:59.395 --> 00:12:02.531
Global cap: a conditional decrement on a counter row.

247
00:12:02.811 --> 00:12:14.075
The candidate checked the hot-row limit against L3 ( about 2,000 per second at high concurrency), proposed 16 bucket rows, and accepted a small over-issue at the end.

248
00:12:14.355 --> 00:12:15.848
36 to 41.

249
00:12:16.128 --> 00:12:16.981
Phase:

250
00:12:17.261 --> 00:12:18.200
Failures.

251
00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:19.397
What happened:

252
00:12:19.677 --> 00:12:21.299
Payment fails, then release.

253
00:12:21.579 --> 00:12:25.205
The reservation carries a TTL, and a sweeper releases it.

254
00:12:25.485 --> 00:12:29.773
Duplicate redeem, then the unique order id returns the prior result.

255
00:12:30.053 --> 00:12:31.632
41 to 45.

256
00:12:31.912 --> 00:12:32.765
Phase:

257
00:12:33.045 --> 00:12:33.899
Curveball.

258
00:12:34.179 --> 00:12:35.096
What happened:

259
00:12:35.376 --> 00:12:40.752
First order per city means a new rule type plus a per-city check against order history.

260
00:12:41.032 --> 00:12:45.235
