319
00:15:08.004 --> 00:15:11.161
Customers should be told when their order status changes.

320
00:15:11.441 --> 00:15:12.337
Design it.

321
00:15:12.617 --> 00:15:13.812
Hidden requirements:

322
00:15:14.092 --> 00:15:18.124
1.5M orders per day, ~8 status changes each.

323
00:15:18.404 --> 00:15:21.817
Channels: push, SMS for critical changes, in-app.

324
00:15:22.097 --> 00:15:24.871
Out for delivery must arrive within 30 s.

325
00:15:25.151 --> 00:15:27.668
No duplicate SMS (they cost money).

326
00:15:27.948 --> 00:15:32.748
Respect quiet hours for promotional messages, but not for transactional ones.

327
00:15:34.098 --> 00:15:35.015
Must-cover:

328
00:15:35.295 --> 00:15:37.855
Event source, then notification pipeline.

329
00:15:38.135 --> 00:15:39.266
Priority lanes.

330
00:15:39.546 --> 00:15:41.146
Dedup or idempotency.

331
00:15:41.426 --> 00:15:42.685
Provider failover.

332
00:15:42.965 --> 00:15:43.797
Chains:

333
00:15:45.147 --> 00:15:53.232
The SMS provider is slow, then retries, then duplicate SMS, then an idempotency key per (order, status, channel).

334
00:15:53.512 --> 00:15:56.819
A burst after a flash sale, then lanes and rate limits.

335
00:15:57.099 --> 00:16:00.043
Ordering: delivered arrives before out for delivery.

336
00:16:00.323 --> 00:16:01.155
Curveball:

337
00:16:01.435 --> 00:16:03.653
Add WhatsApp as a channel in some cities.

338
00:16:03.933 --> 00:16:05.043
Level anchors:

339
00:16:05.323 --> 00:16:09.035
B2: outbox + queue + workers, dedup, retries.

340
00:16:10.385 --> 00:16:14.971
B3: lanes with capacity numbers, provider failover, ordering per order.

341
00:16:16.321 --> 00:16:17.537
Timeline highlights:

342
00:16:17.817 --> 00:16:18.671
Estimate.

343
00:16:18.951 --> 00:16:22.641
1.5M times 8 = 12M events per day.

344
00:16:22.921 --> 00:16:30.239
That averages 140 per second, and a sale peak at 5 times the average is ~700 per second.

345
00:16:30.519 --> 00:16:39.223
Push costs little, SMS costs per message, so the candidate asked which statuses trigger SMS (only delayed and out for delivery).

346
00:16:39.503 --> 00:16:40.463
HLD.

347
00:16:40.743 --> 00:16:45.287
The order service writes an outbox, which feeds Kafka (keyed by order id).

348
00:16:45.567 --> 00:16:54.569
The notification service applies preferences and templates, then puts messages on priority topics, and channel workers send them through providers.

349
00:16:54.849 --> 00:16:58.199
A delivery-status webhook closes the loop (Ch 40).

350
00:16:59.549 --> 00:17:01.149
Deep dive: duplicates.

351
00:17:01.429 --> 00:17:04.586
The idempotency key is (orderId, status, channel).

352
00:17:04.866 --> 00:17:10.989
It is claimed in a table before calling the provider, with the state moving sending, then sent or failed.

353
00:17:11.269 --> 00:17:19.930
If the provider times out, the outcome is unknown: query the provider's status API with the client reference, and don't blindly resend.

354
00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:21.661
Deep dive: ordering.

355
00:17:21.941 --> 00:17:25.311
Events are keyed by order, so they stay in order per partition.

356
00:17:25.591 --> 00:17:30.242
On the device, show the latest status by sequence number and drop stale events.

357
00:17:30.522 --> 00:17:33.573
Transcript excerpt: recovering from a wrong statement.

358
00:17:34.923 --> 00:17:39.680
Candidate:...and Kafka gives us exactly-once, so there are no duplicate SMS.

359
00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:40.856
Interviewer:

360
00:17:41.136 --> 00:17:41.904
Does it?

361
00:17:42.184 --> 00:17:43.101
Candidate:

362
00:17:43.381 --> 00:17:44.576
Let me correct that.

363
00:17:44.856 --> 00:17:48.589
Kafka's exactly-once covers read-process-write within Kafka.

364
00:17:48.869 --> 00:17:54.587
Sending an SMS is an external side effect, so we get at-least-once delivery to our worker.

365
00:17:54.867 --> 00:18:03.741
The duplicate protection has to be our idempotency key before the provider call, plus reconciliation with the provider's delivery reports.

366
00:18:04.021 --> 00:18:09.525
Scorecard excerpt: the quick self-correction counted in the candidate's favour on communication.

367
00:18:09.805 --> 00:18:15.117
It showed they know exactly where Kafka's guarantee ends, which is the real point of the question.

368
00:18:16.467 --> 00:18:19.689
Mock C, Dark-store inventory for 10-minute delivery.

369
00:18:19.969 --> 00:18:20.843
Example:

370
00:18:21.123 --> 00:18:22.745
Interviewer card, Mock C.

371
00:18:23.025 --> 00:18:23.793
Prompt:

372
00:18:24.073 --> 00:18:27.913
We promise delivery in 10 minutes from 400 dark stores.

373
00:18:28.193 --> 00:18:31.606
Design inventory so we never sell what the store doesn't have.

374
00:18:31.886 --> 00:18:33.081
Hidden requirements:

375
00:18:33.361 --> 00:18:35.345
5,000 SKUs per store.

376
00:18:35.625 --> 00:18:39.550
20 orders per minute per store at peak, with about 12 items each.

377
00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:43.755
Stock changes from orders, receiving, damage and cycle counts.

378
00:18:44.035 --> 00:18:49.497
Pickers find items missing about 1.5% of the time, because the counts drift.

379
00:18:50.847 --> 00:18:53.961
Checkout p99 at most 300 ms.

380
00:18:54.241 --> 00:18:56.225
The app shows availability per store.

381
00:18:56.505 --> 00:18:57.423
Must-cover:

382
00:18:57.703 --> 00:18:59.921
Reservation versus physical stock.

383
00:19:00.201 --> 00:19:02.996
Drift and reconciliation with cycle counts.

384
00:19:03.276 --> 00:19:06.881
Availability shown in the app versus availability at checkout.

385
00:19:07.161 --> 00:19:09.231
The hot SKU during a flash deal.

386
00:19:10.581 --> 00:19:11.413
Chains:

387
00:19:11.693 --> 00:19:16.727
Flash deal on one SKU in one store, then hot row, then the L3 numbers.

388
00:19:17.007 --> 00:19:23.535
A picker finds the item missing, then substitution, then correct the count, then feed the ML forecast.

389
00:19:23.815 --> 00:19:28.167
A store goes offline, then the store's own system versus the central one.

390
00:19:28.447 --> 00:19:29.279
Curveball:

391
00:19:29.559 --> 00:19:32.205
We're adding scheduled orders for tomorrow morning.

392
00:19:32.485 --> 00:19:33.594
Level anchors:

393
00:19:34.944 --> 00:19:42.965
B3: a two-level stock model (on-hand versus reservable), a safety buffer derived from drift, and reconciliation loops.

394
00:19:43.245 --> 00:19:49.731
B4: unit economics of buffers versus the cost of missing items, and an org-level ownership model.

395
00:19:51.081 --> 00:19:52.873
The B3 moves that stood out:

396
00:19:53.153 --> 00:20:03.777
A two-number model. onhand is physical stock, updated by receiving, picking and counts. reservable = onhand minus reserved minus safetybuffer.

397
00:20:04.057 --> 00:20:11.054
The buffer is set per SKU from the observed drift, for example, the 95th percentile of count corrections.

398
00:20:12.404 --> 00:20:14.537
Checked the load before over-engineering.

399
00:20:14.817 --> 00:20:27.148
400 stores times 20 orders per minute times 12 items is about 1,600 reservations per second across the fleet, spread over ~2M (store, SKU) rows.

400
00:20:27.428 --> 00:20:30.607
A single-row UPDATE per (store, SKU) is plenty.

401
00:20:30.887 --> 00:20:40.423
Only a flash deal on one row needs attention, and L3 says ~2,000 per second is the ceiling for a single row under heavy concurrency.

402
00:20:40.703 --> 00:20:45.865
The candidate put a per-SKU request gate in front of it rather than sharding every row.

403
00:20:46.145 --> 00:20:48.428
Separated app availability from checkout.

404
00:20:48.708 --> 00:20:53.444
The app reads a cached reservable buffer flag, refreshed from events within seconds.

405
00:20:54.794 --> 00:21:01.322
Checkout reserves against the source of truth, and fails closed with a substitution offer (Ch 46).

406
00:21:01.602 --> 00:21:03.010
Closed the loop on drift.

407
00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:06.895
Every missing found at picking triggers a count task for that bin.

408
00:21:07.175 --> 00:21:10.631
Corrections are events, and the buffer is recomputed weekly.

409
00:21:10.911 --> 00:21:15.498
The metric to watch is the missing-at-pick rate, not stock accuracy in general.

410
00:21:16.848 --> 00:21:18.491
The curveball, done calmly.

411
00:21:18.771 --> 00:21:22.632
Scheduled orders reserve against tomorrow's stock after receiving.

412
00:21:22.912 --> 00:21:30.016
That makes it a forecast-backed reservation with a confirmation step at 06:00 when stock arrives.

413
00:21:30.296 --> 00:21:33.560
It is a separate pool, so it never blocks 10-minute orders.

414
00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:35.824
Transcript excerpt: pushback.

415
00:21:37.174 --> 00:21:38.070
Interviewer:

416
00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:40.249
Why not keep inventory in Redis?

417
00:21:40.529 --> 00:21:41.553
It's faster.

418
00:21:41.833 --> 00:21:42.750
Candidate:

419
00:21:43.030 --> 00:21:53.995
For reservation it would be faster, but then Redis becomes the source of truth for stock, which means persistence, failover semantics and reconciliation back to the warehouse system.

420
00:21:54.275 --> 00:22:01.571
Our load is about 1,600 per second spread over two million rows, which PostgreSQL does comfortably.

421
00:22:01.851 --> 00:22:11.238
I'd put Redis in front only for the one hot-deal SKU as a gate, where losing the gate state is safe because the database still enforces the count.

