Say so.

505
00:27:18.047 --> 00:27:21.482
Compress the phases L1 marks as compressible, aloud.

506
00:27:21.762 --> 00:27:22.743
For example:

507
00:27:23.023 --> 00:27:28.421
I'll state the numbers: ~1,000 validations per second, 50 ms budget.

508
00:27:28.701 --> 00:27:29.319
Or:

509
00:27:29.599 --> 00:27:31.925
Two endpoints: validate and redeem.

510
00:27:32.205 --> 00:27:33.549
Protect the deep dive.

511
00:27:33.829 --> 00:27:36.410
Clarify is not compressible in the same way.

512
00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:40.125
Timebox it at 5 minutes and state your assumptions for the rest.

513
00:27:41.475 --> 00:27:42.883
Calibrating a peer group.

514
00:27:43.163 --> 00:27:48.091
L2 shows that uncalibrated peers disagree on about one verdict in three.

515
00:27:48.371 --> 00:27:53.405
The disagreement comes from bias (lenient or harsh interviewers) as much as noise.

516
00:27:53.685 --> 00:27:54.709
To calibrate:

517
00:27:54.989 --> 00:28:00.728
Anchored rubrics: for each dimension, write down what a 2, 3 and 4 sound like for this prompt.

518
00:28:01.008 --> 00:28:03.632
The level anchors on the cards above are a start.

519
00:28:03.912 --> 00:28:10.291
Shadow scoring: two interviewers score the same recording independently, then compare scores and evidence.

520
00:28:10.571 --> 00:28:14.347
Score review: monthly, look at each interviewer's average score.

521
00:28:14.627 --> 00:28:18.680
A consistently lenient interviewer is a known bias you can correct for.

522
00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:24.062
Evidence over impressions: every score cites a quote or a timestamp.

523
00:28:24.342 --> 00:28:30.550
In L2's model, a single calibrated interviewer decides about as well as four uncalibrated ones.

524
00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:33.070
Calibration is cheaper than more mocks.

525
00:28:34.420 --> 00:28:35.615
Measuring progress.

526
00:28:35.895 --> 00:28:41.249
L4's lesson: one mock score is mostly noise (prompt difficulty and interviewer).

527
00:28:41.529 --> 00:28:46.692
Even with genuine improvement, scores go down about a third of the time over four mocks.

528
00:28:46.972 --> 00:28:47.783
Instead:

529
00:28:48.063 --> 00:28:49.897
Track behaviours, not verdicts.

530
00:28:50.177 --> 00:28:52.119
Did you hit the 20-minute checkpoint?

531
00:28:52.399 --> 00:28:53.679
State the invariant?

532
00:28:53.959 --> 00:28:55.345
Quantify the hot path?

533
00:28:55.625 --> 00:28:56.692
Cover failures?

534
00:28:56.972 --> 00:28:58.359
Each is a yes or no.

535
00:28:58.639 --> 00:29:02.180
Use rolling averages over 3 mocks, and vary the prompts.

536
00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:09.628
Expect about 12 mocks before a trend shows up statistically, which is roughly the number most prep plans use anyway.

537
00:29:10.978 --> 00:29:13.282
Stop mocking when readiness is consistent:

538
00:29:13.562 --> 00:29:17.829
3 consecutive mocks at or above bar, with calibrated interviewers.

539
00:29:19.179 --> 00:29:21.099
Remote and whiteboard logistics.

540
00:29:21.379 --> 00:29:26.733
Practise on the real tool (a shared whiteboard or diagram tool), including drawing speed.

541
00:29:27.013 --> 00:29:29.296
Pre-set a few shapes if the tool allows it.

542
00:29:29.576 --> 00:29:35.123
Camera and audio: a quiet room, a headset, and a second screen for your notes if that's allowed.

543
00:29:35.403 --> 00:29:41.909
Handwriting: use large boxes with few words and label every arrow with the call type (sync or async).

544
