619
00:33:40.929 --> 00:33:42.017
What's real here.

620
00:33:42.297 --> 00:33:49.935
L3 runs against a real PostgreSQL 16 database (fsync=on) on a 2-CPU sandbox.

621
00:33:50.215 --> 00:33:52.711
L1 replays illustrative phase logs.

622
00:33:52.991 --> 00:33:57.727
L2 and L4 are simulations whose assumptions are stated in the code.

623
00:33:58.007 --> 00:34:00.908
Trust their direction, not the exact percentages.

624
00:34:02.258 --> 00:34:06.311
All four labs are JDK 21 single-file programs.

625
00:34:06.591 --> 00:34:19.391
L3 needs the PostgreSQL JDBC driver: java -Duser.timezone=UTC -cp postgresql-42.7.4.jar ClaimCheckL3.java.

626
00:34:20.741 --> 00:34:23.109
L1, Where did the 45 minutes go?

627
00:34:23.389 --> 00:34:24.328
Requirement:

628
00:34:24.608 --> 00:34:27.829
Protect the deep dive, which is where levels are decided.

629
00:34:28.109 --> 00:34:35.064
The program takes six practice mocks as recorded, with each phase's duration and a hard stop at 45 minutes.

630
00:34:35.344 --> 00:34:37.584
It replays them with one checkpoint rule.

631
00:34:38.934 --> 00:34:41.771
Here is the Java code, PacingL1.java.

632
00:34:42.051 --> 00:34:45.785
It runs to 87 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

633
00:34:46.065 --> 00:34:53.339
L1, Pacing: where the 45 minutes actually went in six recorded mocks, and what a checkpoint rule changes.

634
00:34:53.619 --> 00:35:00.275
Phase durations are illustrative, in the pattern typical of practice recordings (not real candidate data).

635
00:35:03.125 --> 00:35:04.043
On screen:

636
00:35:04.323 --> 00:35:05.283
Plan (min):

637
00:35:05.563 --> 00:35:16.336
5 4 4 5 9 12 4 2 total 45. mock (as recorded) clarify or estimate/.../wrap @20min deep+fail overran.

638
00:35:16.616 --> 00:35:25.640
M1 coupon service (B2) 11 7 6 8 10 3 0 0 api 3 or 16 clarify,estimate,data.

639
00:35:25.920 --> 00:35:33.365
M2 order notifications (B2) 6 3 9 7 8 9 3 0 data 12 or 16 api.

640
00:35:33.645 --> 00:35:41.581
M3 dark-store inventory (B3) 5 5 3 6 11 11 4 0 hld 15 or 16 -.

641
00:35:41.861 --> 00:35:49.307
M4 multi-country (B4) 8 2 2 4 12 10 5 2 hld 15 or 16 clarify.

642
00:35:49.587 --> 00:35:56.029
M5 rate limiter (B2) 4 8 5 5 9 10 2 2 data 12 or 16 estimate.

643
00:35:56.309 --> 00:36:03.328
M6 search autocomplete (B3) 7 6 4 9 9 8 2 0 data 10 or 16 data.

644
00:36:03.608 --> 00:36:07.512
Checkpoint rule: at minute 20 you must be drawing the HLD.

645
00:36:07.792 --> 00:36:18.053
If you are behind, compress. estimate or api or data. to their floors (1 or 1 or 2 min: state the numbers, name 2 endpoints, list 3 tables) and.

646
00:36:18.333 --> 00:36:19.848
The rest is in the chapter.

647
00:36:22.698 --> 00:36:23.551
Reading it:

648
00:36:23.831 --> 00:36:24.855
As recorded:

649
00:36:25.135 --> 00:36:29.530
Four of the six mocks were still on the data model or earlier at minute 20.

650
00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:34.290
Deep dive plus failures averaged 11.2 of 16 planned minutes.

651
00:36:34.570 --> 00:36:37.279
M1 got 3 minutes and never reached failures.

652
00:36:37.559 --> 00:36:38.498
With the rule:

653
00:36:38.778 --> 00:36:47.055
HLD by minute 20, compress estimate or API or data aloud if behind raises the average to 15.2.

654
00:36:48.405 --> 00:36:49.813
What the rule can't fix:

655
00:36:50.093 --> 00:36:55.085
M1 still spends 11 minutes clarifying, because clarify is not compressible.

656
00:36:55.365 --> 00:36:57.947
Clarify needs its own 5-minute timebox.

657
00:36:58.227 --> 00:37:02.963
What it doesn't touch: mocks already on time (M3, M4) are unchanged.

658
00:37:04.313 --> 00:37:06.553
L2, Should you trust your peer's verdict?

659
00:37:06.833 --> 00:37:07.771
Requirement:

660
00:37:08.051 --> 00:37:11.870
Know how much a mock verdict tells you, and what makes it more reliable.

661
00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:16.779
The program simulates 200,000 candidates under two interviewer models.

662
00:37:17.059 --> 00:37:20.430
The bias and noise levels are assumptions stated in the code.

663
00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:25.023
Here is the Java code, CalibrationL2.java.

664
00:37:25.303 --> 00:37:29.121
It runs to 76 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

665
00:37:29.401 --> 00:37:37.785
L2, Calibration: how interviewer bias and noise turn into wrong hire decisions, and what calibration and more interviewers buy.

666
00:37:38.065 --> 00:37:49.521
Model (assumptions, VERIFY against your own loop data): candidate skill ~ N(0,1); hire bar = skill 0.5 (~31% should pass).

667
00:37:49.801 --> 00:37:52.959
Interviewer i reports skill + biasi + noise.

668
00:37:53.239 --> 00:37:58.337
Uncalibrated: bias sd 0.5, noise sd 0.6.

669
00:37:58.617 --> 00:38:00.303
Calibrated (anchored rubric.

670
00:38:03.153 --> 00:38:04.070
On screen:

671
00:38:04.350 --> 00:38:18.281
200,000 simulated candidates; hire bar: true skill 0.5 (30.9% should be hired). interviewers k accuracy false hire false reject hire rate 2 agree (k=1).

672
00:38:18.561 --> 00:39:09.258
(uncalibrated panel of 12: mean bias +0.20, most lenient +1.48, harshest -0.85). uncalibrated 1 76.2% 17.0% 6.8% 41.3% 67.7%. uncalibrated 2 81.6% 13.7% 4.7% 39.7% 67.6%. uncalibrated 3 84.5% 12.0% 3.5% 39.2% 67.7%. uncalibrated 4 86.5% 10.8% 2.7% 39.0% 67.7%.

673
00:39:09.538 --> 00:39:54.289
(calibrated panel of 12: mean bias +0.06, most lenient +0.44, harshest -0.26). calibrated 1 86.4% 8.7% 4.8% 34.9% 81.2%. calibrated 2 90.0% 6.4% 3.5% 33.7% 81.0%. calibrated 3 91.7% 5.5% 2.7% 33.5% 81.1%. calibrated 4 93.0% 4.8% 2.2% 33.4% 81.1%.

674
00:39:54.569 --> 00:39:56.084
The rest is in the chapter.

675
00:39:58.934 --> 00:39:59.787
Reading it:

676
00:40:00.067 --> 00:40:06.361
Two uncalibrated interviewers reach the same verdict on only ~68% of candidates.

677
00:40:06.641 --> 00:40:10.097
Calibrated interviewers agree on ~81%.

678
00:40:10.377 --> 00:40:18.825
One calibrated interviewer (86.4%) is as accurate as four uncalibrated ones (86.5%).

679
00:40:19.105 --> 00:40:23.862
Averaging more interviewers reduces the noise, but not the panel's shared lenience.

680
00:40:24.142 --> 00:40:32.163
This simulated panel happened to average +0.20 lenient, which is why false hires outnumber false rejects.

681
00:40:32.443 --> 00:40:38.417
So does the base rate: most candidates sit below the bar, so noise pushes more of them over it than under.

682
00:40:39.767 --> 00:40:48.364
A candidate just above the bar (s = 0.7) gets hire from one mock only 60 to 66% of the time.

683
00:40:48.644 --> 00:40:51.396
One mock's verdict is weak evidence either way.

684
00:40:51.676 --> 00:40:54.428
The numbers depend on the assumed bias and noise.

685
00:40:54.708 --> 00:40:58.655
The direction is the finding: calibrate first, then add interviewers.

686
00:40:58.935 --> 00:41:01.793
VERIFY against your own group's shadow-scoring data.

687
00:41:03.143 --> 00:41:05.319
L3, Check a claim before you make it.

688
00:41:05.599 --> 00:41:06.538
Requirement:

689
00:41:06.818 --> 00:41:14.050
In Mocks A and C I'll say one hot stock or coupon row handles about 2,000 reservations per second.

690
00:41:14.330 --> 00:41:15.311
Is that true?

691
00:41:15.591 --> 00:41:25.597
This lab runs against real PostgreSQL 16 with fsync=on, from 1, 8 and 32 concurrent connections, for 4 seconds per run.

692
00:41:26.947 --> 00:41:29.891
Here is the Java code, ClaimCheckL3.java.

693
00:41:30.171 --> 00:41:33.776
It runs to 82 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

694
00:41:34.056 --> 00:41:45.853
L3, Check a claim before you make it in a mock: one conditional UPDATE on a single stock row handles a. flash sale of 2,000 reservations per second for one SKU.

695
00:41:46.133 --> 00:41:51.979
Real PostgreSQL 16 (fsync=on), 2-CPU sandbox, localhost.

696
00:41:52.259 --> 00:41:53.048
Modes:

697
00:41:53.328 --> 00:42:01.499
A single row; B 16 bucket rows (random bucket, move on if empty); C single row, 20 reservations per commit (batched).

698
00:42:04.349 --> 00:42:05.266
On screen:

699
00:42:05.546 --> 00:42:06.421
Each run:

700
00:42:06.701 --> 00:42:10.562
4 s of closed-loop load, stock large enough never to run out.

701
00:42:10.842 --> 00:42:12.357
Target from. the mock:

702
00:42:12.637 --> 00:42:20.253
2,000 reservations per second. mode threads reservations per second p50 ms p99 ms.

703
00:42:20.533 --> 00:42:26.719
A single row 1 4,398 0.19 0.81.

704
00:42:26.999 --> 00:42:32.695
A single row 8 3,722 1.54 9.67.

705
00:42:32.975 --> 00:42:40.058
A single row 32 1,976 10.66 76.56.

706
00:42:40.338 --> 00:42:47.762
B 16 bucket rows 1 5,297 0.17 0.46.

707
00:42:48.042 --> 00:42:54.954
B 16 bucket rows 8 10,844 0.60 2.72.

708
00:42:55.234 --> 00:43:02.402
B 16 bucket rows 32 9,643 2.44 14.61.

709
00:43:02.682 --> 00:43:09.679
C single row, 20 per commit 1 97,040 0.18 0.53.

710
00:43:09.959 --> 00:43:17.085
C single row, 20 per commit 8 72,340 1.52 10.11.

711
00:43:17.365 --> 00:43:24.895
C single row, 20 per commit 32 41,020 10.39 74.44.

712
00:43:25.175 --> 00:43:26.690
The rest is in the chapter.

713
00:43:29.540 --> 00:43:30.393
Reading it:

714
00:43:30.673 --> 00:43:33.916
Single row: throughput falls as concurrency rises:

715
00:43:34.196 --> 00:43:48.212
4,398 per second with one connection, 3,722 per second with 8, and 1,976 per second with 32, with p99 77 ms.

716
00:43:48.492 --> 00:43:51.223
Every transaction queues on the same row lock.

717
00:43:51.503 --> 00:43:56.708
So the claim holds only at the edge, and it breaks a 50 ms p99 budget.

718
00:43:56.988 --> 00:44:08.487
A second run gave 2,313 per second and a 69 ms p99 at 32 connections, so expect ±15% between runs.

719
00:44:09.837 --> 00:44:20.546
16 bucket rows: lock contention spreads out, giving 10,844 per second at 8 connections with a 2.7 ms p99.

720
00:44:20.826 --> 00:44:31.045
Going from 8 to 32 connections adds nothing (9,643 per second), which is consistent with the 2-CPU sandbox being the limit.

721
00:44:31.325 --> 00:44:40.370
Batching 20 per commit: throughput is enormous (97k per second with one connection), but each reservation waits for its batch's commit.

722
00:44:40.650 --> 00:44:44.639
The latency pattern follows the lock queue, as in the single-row case.

723
00:44:45.989 --> 00:44:47.035
In the mock, say:

724
00:44:47.315 --> 00:44:54.355
About 2,000 per second on one hot row under real concurrency, with a p99 of tens of milliseconds.

725
00:44:54.635 --> 00:44:57.707
Above that, I'd use bucket rows or a gate in front.

726
00:44:57.987 --> 00:45:02.040
Hardware differs, so quote the order of magnitude and say you'd measure.

727
00:45:03.390 --> 00:45:05.502
L4, Am I actually improving?

728
00:45:05.782 --> 00:45:06.721
Requirement:

729
00:45:07.001 --> 00:45:11.075
Decide from my mock scores whether I'm improving, without fooling myself.

730
00:45:11.355 --> 00:45:22.619
The simulation adds noise from prompt difficulty and the interviewer (total sd about 0.5 on the 1 to 4 scale; an assumption) on top of a true per-mock gain.

731
00:45:23.969 --> 00:45:26.956
Here is the Java code, ProgressL4.java.

732
00:45:27.236 --> 00:45:30.649
It runs to 50 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

733
00:45:30.929 --> 00:45:33.895
L4, Are my mocks improving, or is it noise?

734
00:45:34.175 --> 00:45:36.457
Scores on the 1 to 4 rubric scale.

735
00:45:36.737 --> 00:45:56.044
Model (assumption, VERIFY with your own spread): true level starts at 2.4 and improves by gain per mock;. each mock score = true level + prompt difficulty (sd 0.35) + interviewer (sd 0.35), then total noise sd about 0.5.

736
00:45:58.894 --> 00:45:59.811
On screen:

737
00:46:00.091 --> 00:46:03.718
How often does a series of N mocks show what is really happening?

738
00:46:03.998 --> 00:47:17.927
(50,000 simulated. candidates per cell). gain or mock N last first slope 0 (90%) rolling-3 rose true gain over N. +0.00 4 50% 10% 50% +0.00. +0.00 6 50% 10% 50% +0.00. +0.00 8 50% 10% 50% +0.00. +0.00 12 50% 10% 50% +0.00. +0.05 4 41% 14% 59% +0.15. +0.05 6 36% 19% 64% +0.25. +0.05 8 31% 25% 73% +0.35. +0.05 12 22% 45% 87% +0.55. +0.10 4 33% 18% 67% +0.30.

739
00:47:18.207 --> 00:47:19.722
The rest is in the chapter.

740
00:47:22.572 --> 00:47:23.425
Reading it:

741
00:47:23.705 --> 00:47:27.545
Genuine improvement still looks like decline surprisingly often.

742
00:47:27.825 --> 00:47:37.596
At +0.10 per mock, 33% of candidates score lower on mock 4 than on mock 1, and 16% still do on mock 8.

743
00:47:37.876 --> 00:47:45.620
A statistically clear trend needs about 12 mocks (85% detection at +0.10 per mock).

744
00:47:45.900 --> 00:47:50.615
With no real improvement, the test fires 10% of the time, as designed.

745
00:47:51.965 --> 00:47:59.965
Rolling 3-mock averages point the right way more often (89% at N = 8, gain +0.10).

746
00:48:00.245 --> 00:48:04.533
With no gain they rise half the time, so they indicate direction, not proof.

747
00:48:04.813 --> 00:48:10.295
For N = 4 the windows overlap, and the comparison reduces to last versus first.

748
00:48:10.575 --> 00:48:17.530
Practical rule: track yes or no behaviours (checkpoint hit, invariant stated, failures covered), which you control.

749
00:48:17.810 --> 00:48:20.498
Treat any single score as one noisy sample.

750
00:48:21.848 --> 00:48:22.787
Part 6.

751
00:48:23.067 --> 00:48:24.347
Practice Concerns.

752
00:48:25.597 --> 00:48:26.727
Choosing prompts.

753
00:48:27.007 --> 00:48:32.746
Mix familiar and unfamiliar prompts: about 60% from Part V, 40% new.

754
00:48:33.026 --> 00:48:35.671
Include one prompt per week from outside your domain.

755
00:48:35.951 --> 00:48:41.925
For staff and principal loops, include at least one evolution or strategy prompt, like Mock D.

756
00:48:43.275 --> 00:48:44.917
Being a good mock interviewer.

757
00:48:45.197 --> 00:48:47.821
Stick to the card and don't rescue the candidate.

758
00:48:48.101 --> 00:48:49.488
Pull chains to the edge.

759
00:48:49.768 --> 00:48:51.176
Score before you talk.

760
00:48:51.456 --> 00:48:56.533
Being an interviewer also makes you a better candidate: you see the rubric from the other side.

761
00:48:57.883 --> 00:48:59.398
Recording and privacy.

762
00:48:59.678 --> 00:49:00.915
Record with consent.

763
00:49:01.195 --> 00:49:05.398
Keep recordings private to the group, and delete them after the prep period.

764
00:49:05.678 --> 00:49:09.497
Never use real proprietary designs from employers in mocks.

765
00:49:10.847 --> 00:49:12.340
Company-specific styles.

766
00:49:12.620 --> 00:49:23.052
Formats differ between companies: whiteboard versus doc-based design, product versus infrastructure focus, strict 45 minutes versus a 60-minute deep dive.

767
00:49:23.332 --> 00:49:25.615
Adjust your pacing checkpoints to the format.

768
00:49:25.895 --> 00:49:27.665
Ask your recruiter about the format.

769
00:49:27.945 --> 00:49:29.545
This is expected and fine.

770
00:49:30.895 --> 00:49:31.727
Burnout.

771
00:49:32.007 --> 00:49:33.735
Cap mocks at 3 to 4 a week.

772
00:49:34.015 --> 00:49:37.813
Rest days help consolidation (Ch 59 L4).

773
00:49:38.093 --> 00:49:40.418
Do a light drill the day before, not a mock.

774
00:49:41.768 --> 00:49:42.707
Part 7.

775
00:49:42.987 --> 00:49:44.693
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

776
00:49:45.943 --> 00:49:46.861
Watch out:

777
00:49:47.141 --> 00:49:47.781
1.

778
00:49:48.061 --> 00:49:49.149
Friendly mocks.

779
00:49:49.429 --> 00:49:51.605
No clock, no follow-ups, no score.

780
00:49:51.885 --> 00:49:53.783
It feels good and teaches little.

781
00:49:55.133 --> 00:49:56.051
Watch out:

782
00:49:56.331 --> 00:49:57.035
2.

783
00:49:57.315 --> 00:49:58.701
Rescuing the candidate.

784
00:49:58.981 --> 00:50:04.400
The interviewer reveals hidden requirements early, so the requirements skill never gets tested.

785
00:50:05.750 --> 00:50:06.667
Watch out:

786
00:50:06.947 --> 00:50:07.694
3.

787
00:50:07.974 --> 00:50:09.339
Scoring after the chat.

788
00:50:09.619 --> 00:50:11.454
The discussion softens the score.

789
00:50:11.734 --> 00:50:13.270
Fill the scorecard first.

790
00:50:14.620 --> 00:50:15.537
Watch out:

791
00:50:15.817 --> 00:50:16.521
4.

792
00:50:16.801 --> 00:50:18.359
Judging by one verdict.

793
00:50:18.639 --> 00:50:21.007
One mock is noise (L2, L4).

794
00:50:22.357 --> 00:50:23.274
Watch out:

795
00:50:23.554 --> 00:50:24.322
5.

796
00:50:24.602 --> 00:50:25.711
Vague debriefs.

797
00:50:25.991 --> 00:50:27.805
Be more structured changes nothing.

798
00:50:28.085 --> 00:50:29.642
Name specific behaviours.

799
00:50:30.992 --> 00:50:31.909
Watch out:

800
00:50:32.189 --> 00:50:32.936
6.

801
00:50:33.216 --> 00:50:34.752
Always the same partner.

802
00:50:35.032 --> 00:50:36.888
Their bias becomes your baseline.

803
00:50:38.238 --> 00:50:39.155
Watch out:

804
00:50:39.435 --> 00:50:40.182
7.

805
00:50:40.462 --> 00:50:41.913
Only familiar prompts.

806
00:50:42.193 --> 00:50:44.603
You end up rehearsing answers, not thinking.

807
00:50:45.953 --> 00:50:46.871
Watch out:

808
00:50:47.151 --> 00:50:47.833
8.

809
00:50:48.113 --> 00:50:49.521
Unchecked standard claims.

810
00:50:49.801 --> 00:50:53.727
Redis handles anything and Kafka is exactly-once get probed.

811
00:50:54.007 --> 00:50:55.841
Measure or look them up (L3).

812
00:50:57.191 --> 00:50:58.109
Watch out:

813
00:50:58.389 --> 00:50:59.135
9.

814
00:50:59.415 --> 00:51:00.482
No recording.

815
00:51:00.762 --> 00:51:03.194
You can't see your own pacing or filler words.

816
00:51:04.544 --> 00:51:05.461
Watch out:

817
00:51:05.741 --> 00:51:06.445
10.

818
00:51:06.725 --> 00:51:08.347
Ignoring the curveball skill.

819
00:51:08.627 --> 00:51:10.781
Real interviewers change requirements.

820
00:51:11.061 --> 00:51:13.109
Practise minimal, calm changes.

821
00:51:14.459 --> 00:51:15.377
Watch out:

822
00:51:15.657 --> 00:51:16.489
11.

823
00:51:16.769 --> 00:51:18.347
Mocking the day before the loop.

824
00:51:18.627 --> 00:51:20.398
It adds fatigue and anxiety.

825
00:51:20.678 --> 00:51:22.107
Do a light drill instead.

826
00:51:23.457 --> 00:51:24.375
Watch out:

827
00:51:24.655 --> 00:51:25.444
12.

828
00:51:25.724 --> 00:51:28.412
Staff-level candidates only drawing boxes.

829
00:51:28.692 --> 00:51:32.873
At B4, sequencing, cost and ownership are the signal (Mock D).

830
00:51:34.223 --> 00:51:35.098
Part 8.

831
00:51:35.378 --> 00:51:36.722
Interview Q and A.

832
00:51:37.972 --> 00:51:38.804
Beginner.

833
00:51:39.084 --> 00:51:40.108
Question 1.

834
00:51:40.388 --> 00:51:43.567
What makes a mock interview useful rather than just practice?

835
00:51:43.847 --> 00:51:45.212
Thirty-second answer:

836
00:51:45.492 --> 00:51:59.764
Realistic conditions (clock, stranger-like interviewer, hidden requirements, follow-ups), a level-anchored scorecard with evidence, a recording, and a debrief that ends in two specific behavioural changes, tracked across mocks.

837
00:52:01.114 --> 00:52:02.117
Question 2.

838
00:52:02.397 --> 00:52:05.383
How should you use the first five minutes of a design interview?

839
00:52:05.663 --> 00:52:07.029
Thirty-second answer:

840
00:52:07.309 --> 00:52:19.575
Clarify users, core features, scale, latency and consistency needs, and non-goals; write them down; then state the assumptions you'll use for anything unanswered, and move on by minute five.

841
00:52:20.925 --> 00:52:21.971
Question 3.

842
00:52:22.251 --> 00:52:24.896
What do you do when you don't know something in an interview?

843
00:52:25.176 --> 00:52:26.541
Thirty-second answer:

844
00:52:26.821 --> 00:52:35.845
Say so, reason from principles to an estimate, and say how you'd verify it, I don't know the exact figure; roughly N because...; I'd measure it with....

845
00:52:36.125 --> 00:52:37.043
Never bluff.

846
00:52:38.393 --> 00:52:39.459
Question 4.

847
00:52:39.739 --> 00:52:42.107
How do you practise system design on your own?

848
00:52:42.387 --> 00:52:43.753
Thirty-second answer:

849
00:52:44.033 --> 00:52:56.449
Timed, recorded solo mocks on a whiteboard tool, answering aloud from a prompt card, then reviewing the recording for pacing and clarity; plus daily Q and A drills and one design written up per week.

850
00:52:57.799 --> 00:52:58.801
Intermediate.

851
00:52:59.081 --> 00:53:00.191
Question 5.

852
00:53:00.471 --> 00:53:03.713
You're 20 minutes in and still haven't drawn the architecture.

853
00:53:03.993 --> 00:53:04.996
What do you do?

854
00:53:05.276 --> 00:53:06.641
Thirty-second answer:

855
00:53:06.921 --> 00:53:22.495
Say it aloud, compress estimates or API or data to one line each (~1,000 validations per second; validate and redeem endpoints; coupon and redemption tables), draw the HLD now, and protect the deep-dive time.

856
00:53:23.845 --> 00:53:24.954
Question 6.

857
00:53:25.234 --> 00:53:29.053
The interviewer asks 'why not use X instead?' How do you respond?

858
00:53:29.333 --> 00:53:30.698
Thirty-second answer:

859
00:53:30.978 --> 00:53:42.477
Restate the requirement that drove your choice, compare X honestly on it, then either keep your choice with the reason or switch with the reason, both are fine; flip-flopping without reasons isn't.

860
00:53:43.827 --> 00:53:44.915
Question 7.

861
00:53:45.195 --> 00:53:48.181
How do you handle a requirement change at minute 30?

862
00:53:48.461 --> 00:53:49.827
Thirty-second answer:

863
00:53:50.107 --> 00:54:02.736
Identify what the change affects (data, APIs, invariants, load), make the minimal modification, re-check the invariants and failure cases touched, and state what you'd revisit with more time.

864
00:54:04.086 --> 00:54:05.110
Question 8.

865
00:54:05.390 --> 00:54:09.763
How do you make a claim like 'a single database row can handle this' credible?

866
00:54:10.043 --> 00:54:11.409
Thirty-second answer:

867
00:54:11.689 --> 00:54:34.835
Give the number and its basis, measured or well-known, and the condition: for example, a conditional UPDATE on one row did ~4,400 per second from one connection but ~2,000 per second with a 77 ms p99 at 32 concurrent callers on a small box; then say what you'd do if the load is higher (buckets, batching, a gate).

868
00:54:36.185 --> 00:54:37.252
Question 9.

869
00:54:37.532 --> 00:54:39.196
How should a mock debrief work?

870
00:54:39.476 --> 00:54:40.841
Thirty-second answer:

871
00:54:41.121 --> 00:54:51.745
Candidate self-assessment, the timeline, a scorecard with evidence quotes, a check of every claim made, and two specific behavioural changes, logged and checked next mock.

872
00:54:53.095 --> 00:54:53.863
Senior.

873
00:54:54.143 --> 00:54:55.189
Question 10.

874
00:54:55.469 --> 00:55:00.269
In the dark-store inventory mock, how do you prevent selling items that aren't physically there?

875
00:55:00.549 --> 00:55:01.914
Thirty-second answer:

876
00:55:02.194 --> 00:55:20.029
Separate on-hand from reservable stock with a per-SKU safety buffer derived from count drift; reserve at checkout against the source of truth; show cached availability in the app; and every missing-at-pick triggers a count and feeds buffer recalculation, measured by missing-at-pick rate.

877
00:55:21.379 --> 00:55:22.509
Question 11.

878
00:55:22.789 --> 00:55:26.160
Why do peer mock verdicts disagree, and what do you do about it?

879
00:55:26.440 --> 00:55:27.805
Thirty-second answer:

880
00:55:28.085 --> 00:55:37.472
Interviewer bias (lenient or harsh) and noise; in a simple model two uncalibrated peers agreed on only ~68% of verdicts.

881
00:55:37.752 --> 00:55:45.581
Anchored rubrics, shadow scoring on shared recordings, evidence-based scores and reviewing each interviewer's average fix most of it.

882
00:55:46.931 --> 00:55:48.019
Question 12.

883
00:55:48.299 --> 00:55:51.286
How do you design an interviewer card for a new prompt?

884
00:55:51.566 --> 00:55:52.931
Thirty-second answer:

885
00:55:53.211 --> 00:56:08.230
A one-line prompt, hidden requirements that change the design, 2 to 3 must-cover hard parts, 3 to 4 follow-up chains each 3 to 4 links deep, one curveball, level anchors for B2 or B3 or B4, and the scorecard.

886
00:56:09.580 --> 00:56:10.860
Question 13.

887
00:56:11.140 --> 00:56:15.705
How do you avoid the 'Kafka is exactly-once' trap in a notification design?

888
00:56:15.985 --> 00:56:17.351
Thirty-second answer:

889
00:56:17.631 --> 00:56:36.425
Kafka's exactly-once covers read-process-write within Kafka; an SMS send is an external side effect, so the consumer sees at-least-once; protect with an idempotency key claimed before the provider call, status queries for unknown outcomes, and reconciliation with delivery reports.

890
00:56:37.775 --> 00:56:38.970
Question 14.

891
00:56:39.250 --> 00:56:42.045
How do you know your mock scores are actually improving?

892
00:56:42.325 --> 00:56:43.690
Thirty-second answer:

893
00:56:43.970 --> 00:57:01.079
Single scores are noisy, in a simple model a third of genuinely improving candidates score lower on mock 4 than mock 1; track yes or no behaviours per mock, rolling averages over varied prompts, and expect around 12 mocks before a trend is statistically clear.

894
00:57:02.429 --> 00:57:03.325
Architect.

895
00:57:03.605 --> 00:57:04.800
Question 15.

896
00:57:05.080 --> 00:57:09.133
How do you approach 'launch in a second country' in a principal-level interview?

897
00:57:09.413 --> 00:57:10.779
Thirty-second answer:

898
00:57:11.059 --> 00:57:31.624
Frame it as product, org and architecture; name hard constraints (residency, payments, cost) and one-way doors; propose country cells from the same code with market configuration as data; estimate the cell's fixed cost; define ownership; sequence reversible steps with metrics and rollback; and say what you won't build yet.

899
00:57:32.974 --> 00:57:34.169
Question 16.

900
00:57:34.449 --> 00:57:39.163
What distinguishes a B4 answer to a strategy prompt from a B3 answer?

901
00:57:39.443 --> 00:57:40.809
Thirty-second answer:

902
00:57:41.089 --> 00:57:56.747
B3 designs a sound regional deployment; B4 also sequences the work, quantifies cost and risk, sets ownership and decision records, identifies what not to do, and makes the first steps reversible, the boxes take a quarter of the time.

903
00:57:58.097 --> 00:57:59.441
Question 17.

904
00:57:59.721 --> 00:58:02.324
You're running interview calibration for an org.

905
00:58:02.604 --> 00:58:03.927
What do you put in place?

906
00:58:04.207 --> 00:58:05.572
Thirty-second answer:

907
00:58:05.852 --> 00:58:21.873
Anchored rubrics per level and prompt, interviewer training with shadowing, evidence-based written feedback before debriefs, periodic score audits per interviewer, and outcome tracking (performance of hires), the same levers as the peer mock programme, at org scale.

908
00:58:23.223 --> 00:58:24.397
Question 18.

909
00:58:24.677 --> 00:58:29.349
How do you use mocks to prepare for leadership and behavioural rounds, not just design?

910
00:58:29.629 --> 00:58:30.994
Thirty-second answer:

911
00:58:31.274 --> 00:58:32.341
Mock those too:

912
00:58:32.621 --> 00:58:47.469
5 to 8 stories in an evidence format, delivered to a peer who probes for your specific actions, trade-offs and outcomes; score for scope, judgement and influence; and tie stories to design topics (a migration, an incident).

913
00:58:48.819 --> 00:58:50.056
Question 19.

914
00:58:50.336 --> 00:58:54.069
In a mock, the interviewer challenges a trade-off you're confident about.

915
00:58:54.349 --> 00:58:56.184
How do you handle it at staff level?

916
00:58:56.464 --> 00:58:57.829
Thirty-second answer:

917
00:58:58.109 --> 00:59:12.232
Engage the strongest version of their alternative, restate the requirement and the costs on both sides with numbers, identify the condition under which you'd flip, and keep your choice if it still holds, a calm disagreement is a staff signal.

918
00:59:13.582 --> 00:59:14.691
Question 20.

919
00:59:14.971 --> 00:59:19.323
What would you change about how most engineers prepare for system design interviews?

920
00:59:19.603 --> 00:59:20.969
Thirty-second answer:

921
00:59:21.249 --> 00:59:35.649
Less reading, more timed spoken practice; measured claims rather than folklore; calibrated scoring instead of friendly verdicts; tracking behaviours instead of single scores; and preparing strategy prompts and stories for senior levels.

922
00:59:36.999 --> 00:59:37.895
Part 9.

923
00:59:38.175 --> 00:59:39.583
Summary and Practice.

924
00:59:40.833 --> 00:59:41.921
Key takeaways.

925
00:59:42.201 --> 00:59:45.742
Mocks need a card, a clock, a scorecard and a debrief.

926
00:59:46.022 --> 00:59:47.323
Pace with checkpoints.

927
00:59:47.603 --> 00:59:51.081
HLD by minute 20 protects the deep dive (L1).

928
00:59:51.361 --> 00:59:54.539
Calibrate interviewers before trusting verdicts (L2).

929
00:59:54.819 --> 00:59:57.913
Check your standard claims against real numbers (L3).

930
00:59:58.193 --> 01:00:01.329
Track behaviours and trends, not one score (L4).

931
01:00:01.609 --> 01:00:06.089
At B4, strategy, sequencing and ownership are the signal (Mock D).

932
01:00:07.439 --> 01:00:08.420
Cheat sheet.

933
01:00:08.700 --> 01:00:10.044
Here is a quick table:

934
01:00:10.324 --> 01:00:11.519
Moment, and Move.

935
01:00:11.799 --> 01:00:12.759
Minute 5:

936
01:00:13.039 --> 01:00:15.385
Stop clarifying, state your assumptions.

937
01:00:15.665 --> 01:00:16.647
Minute 20:

938
01:00:16.927 --> 01:00:19.380
HLD on the board, or compress aloud.

939
01:00:19.660 --> 01:00:20.407
Stuck:

940
01:00:20.687 --> 01:00:22.927
Restate the requirement, list the options.

941
01:00:23.207 --> 01:00:24.017
Pushback:

942
01:00:24.297 --> 01:00:27.817
Requirement, then compare, then keep or switch with a reason.

943
01:00:28.097 --> 01:00:28.972
Unknown:

944
01:00:29.252 --> 01:00:31.471
Estimate, then say how you'd verify it.

945
01:00:31.751 --> 01:00:32.583
Curveball:

946
01:00:32.863 --> 01:00:35.337
Minimal change, re-check the invariants.

947
01:00:35.617 --> 01:00:36.428
After:

948
01:00:36.708 --> 01:00:38.905
Scorecard first, then two changes.

949
01:00:40.255 --> 01:00:41.386
Exercises.

950
01:00:41.666 --> 01:00:46.914
(B1) Record a solo mock of Mock A and log your phase times with L1's format.

951
01:00:47.194 --> 01:00:50.863
(B2) Run Mock B with a peer as interviewer, using the card.

952
01:00:51.143 --> 01:00:53.469
Debrief with §2.10.

953
01:00:53.749 --> 01:00:56.159
(B3) Change L3 to use SELECT...

954
01:00:56.439 --> 01:00:57.911
FOR UPDATE then UPDATE.

955
01:00:58.191 --> 01:00:59.855
How much slower is it, and why?

956
01:01:00.135 --> 01:01:04.594
(B3) Write an interviewer card for a prompt from Part V that isn't used here.

957
01:01:04.874 --> 01:01:09.802
(B4) Run Mock D and write the one-page decision record the answer implies.

958
01:01:11.152 --> 01:01:12.155
Further reading.

959
01:01:12.435 --> 01:01:18.941
Books and guides on structured interviewing and interviewer calibration (VERIFY which your target company uses).

